<h3 class="synohw-sub">Fan</h3>

_(Fan table)_ (FAN_MODE):
: <select name="FAN_MODE" aria-label="Fan table (FAN_MODE)">
  <?=mk_option($cfg['FAN_MODE'], "LOW", "LOW: DUAL_MODE_LOW (quieter, plugin default)")?>
  <?=mk_option($cfg['FAN_MODE'], "HIGH", "HIGH: DUAL_MODE_HIGH (cooler, louder)")?>
  </select>

:synohw_fan_mode_plug:
> **What it does.** Chooses which of the two fan tables that Synology ships for the DS2415+ the daemon follows. Both are in DSM's own `scemd.xml`, captured from this unit's DSM 7.1.1 (`capture/2026-10-23/bin/usr/syno/etc.defaults/scemd.xml`). The plugin copies them, it does not invent a curve. The values in °C are temperatures at or above which a row applies:
>
> - **LOW** (`DUAL_MODE_LOW`, scemd.xml:17-29): disk from 0 / 46 / 50 / 54 °C &rarr; 20 / 40 / 70 / 99 %; disk ALERT 58 °C (LEDs, fan stays 99 %); disk SHUTDOWN band 61 °C.
> - **HIGH** (`DUAL_MODE_HIGH`, scemd.xml:3-15): disk from 0 / 41 / 45 / 49 °C &rarr; 20 / 40 / 70 / 99 %; disk ALERT 53 °C; disk SHUTDOWN band 61 °C.
> - **CPU, same in both:** from 0 / 65 / 85 °C &rarr; 20 / 70 / 99 %; CPU ALERT 85 °C; CPU SHUTDOWN band 95 °C.
>
> **How the duty is chosen.** Disk input = the hottest real disk in Unraid's `/var/local/emhttp/disks.ini` (array and pool slots) and `devs.ini` (unassigned devices). Not disks, so skipped: empty slots (`status` starting with `DISK_NP`, or `device=""`, as the webGui does: `nchan/device_list:185-186`), the flash, and every USB device (the DOM, a stick: decided from `/sys/block/<dev>/device`, which leads through `/usb` for them; `sdX` names change between boots so they are never used to decide). A disk asleep shows `temp="*"` with `spundown="1"` and counts as cool (step 0), so no disk is ever woken. A disk that shows `temp="*"` with `spundown="0"` is spinning without a reading: it is **not** counted as cool but as unreadable, so the 70 % floor applies, and it is not probed either.
>
> **Why `*` + spundown 0 is "unreadable".** Measured on this DS2415+ on 2026-10-25 01:52, array unassigned: disks.ini held only the 24 empty slots (`device=""` `status="DISK_NP"` `temp="*"`) and the flash; the two HDDs were in devs.ini (`sdd` `spundown="0"` `temp="28"`, `sdc` `spundown="0"` `temp="31"`) next to the USB DOM `sdb` with `temp="*"`. Version 2026.10.25b read only disks.ini, took all those `*` for sleeping disks and held the fan at 20 % whatever the HDDs did. A spinning disk without a temperature could be hot, so the plugin assumes the worse case.
>
> CPU input = the hottest `coretemp` sensor in `/sys/class/hwmon`. Duty = the higher of the two results. One input unreadable: at least 70 % (the plugin's "MIDDLE" floor). Both unreadable: 99 % ("FULL"). The daemon steps down only when the temperature is 2 °C below the row it is on (every scemd row has `threshold="2"`). It reads the CPU every 30 s (scemd `period="30"`) and disks.ini and devs.ini every 60 s (scemd uses `period="300"` for disks; the files cost nothing to read).
>
> **Bytes sent.** `V<nn>` (fan duty in %, e.g. `V20`, `V99`) whenever the result changes and again every 300 s in case the MCU was reset; `W20` once at start (PWM 20 Hz: every row says `NN%20hz`). In the ALERT band `;` (status LED orange blinking) and `LA1` (alert LED on); when it clears `8` (green) and `LA3`. All measured under DSM on this unit (`research/2026-10-23-microp-commands.md`).
>
> **Default LOW, and why.** It is the quieter of Synology's two tables for this model. The plugin README and design notes call LOW "the DSM default"; nothing captured from DSM proves which table DSM picks out of the box (`synoinfo.conf:180` only says `support_fan_adjust_dual_mode="yes"`), so treat that as not verified.
>
> **Not measured.** Which disk temperature DSM itself feeds into the table (hottest disk is the plugin's choice) and which CPU sensor scemd reads. The fan speed is not read back: there is no tach reading, only the duty that was sent.
>
> **Risk.** LOW lets disks reach 54 °C before the fan is at 99 %; HIGH reaches 99 % at 49 °C and is louder. Neither can stop the fan: the lowest row is 20 % and the allow-list refuses `V` below 10. Changing it restarts the daemon (Apply): a few hundred milliseconds without keepalive, startup bytes sent again. "What each table would do" under Fan control on this tab shows what each table would choose at the current temperatures.
:end

_(DS2415+ fan mode)_ (DS_MODE):
: <select name="DS_MODE" aria-label="DS2415+ fan mode (DS_MODE)">
  <?=mk_option($cfg['DS_MODE'], "AUTO", "AUTO: follow the fan table (plugin default)")?>
  <?=mk_option($cfg['DS_MODE'], "MANUAL", "MANUAL: fixed duty, safety rules still raise it")?>
  </select>

:synohw_ds_mode_plug:
> **AUTO** (default): the daemon follows the fan table chosen above, as DSM does. **MANUAL**: it sends the duty set below whatever the temperatures, **except** that the plugin's safety rules still raise it (never lower it): a temperature in the ALERT band (disk 58 °C LOW / 53 °C HIGH, CPU 85 °C) gives 99 %, one unreadable source or a spinning disk without a reading gives at least 70 % (`UNREADABLE_FLOOR`), no temperature at all gives 99 % (`NO_DATA_DUTY`). The SHUTDOWN band acts as in AUTO (see "Power off in the SHUTDOWN band").
>
> **Risk.** A low MANUAL duty lets the disks run warmer than either Synology table would allow until the ALERT band; the table rows in between (40 / 70 / 99 % from 46 / 50 / 54 °C) are not applied. The Overview card and Fan control on this tab say which rule is in force.
:end

_(DS2415+ lowest duty in AUTO, %)_ (DS_FLOOR):
: <input type="number" name="DS_FLOOR" aria-label="DS2415+ lowest duty in AUTO, % (DS_FLOOR)" min="10" max="20" value="<?=synohw_h($cfg['DS_FLOOR'])?>">

:synohw_ds_floor_plug:
> **What it does.** In AUTO, replaces the duty of the table's lowest row (both tables, disk and CPU: 20 % in `scemd.xml`) by this value, 10..20. Every other row, the hysteresis and the floors stay as they are. Default 20 = what DSM does.
>
> **Measured on this unit** (fan sweep of 2026-10-25, idle; the summary was handed over by the main session, the sweep files are on the NAS flash and not in this repository): duty 10 % settled at CPU 37-38 °C / disks 33 °C; 20 % at 33 / 31 °C; 99 % at 27 / 28 °C. So 10 % costs about 4-5 °C on the CPU and 2 °C on the disks at idle compared with DSM's 20 %.
>
> **Caveats.** The fan speed cannot be read (no tach): whether the fan really turns slower at 10-19 % than at 20 % is inferred from the temperatures only, and a duty the fan cannot start at could stall it unnoticed. Load (parity check, transcoding) raises the temperatures above those idle numbers; the table rows still raise the fan when they are reached.
:end

_(DS2415+ manual duty, %)_ (DS_DUTY):
: <input type="number" name="DS_DUTY" aria-label="DS2415+ manual duty, % (DS_DUTY)" min="10" max="99" value="<?=synohw_h($cfg['DS_DUTY'])?>">

:synohw_ds_duty_plug:
> Used only with DS2415+ fan mode MANUAL: the duty sent (`V<nn>`), 10..99. The allow-list refuses anything below 10. Same measured numbers and caveats as the lowest-duty setting above.
:end

<h3 class="synohw-sub">Front-panel MCU (ttyS1)</h3>

_(Keepalive interval, seconds)_ (KEEPALIVE_SEC):
: <input type="number" name="KEEPALIVE_SEC" aria-label="Keepalive interval, seconds (KEEPALIVE_SEC)" min="2" max="60" value="<?=synohw_h($cfg['KEEPALIVE_SEC'])?>">

:synohw_keepalive_plug:
> **What it does.** How often the daemon sends `u` (DSM name `ENABLE_FAN_CHECKER`) to the MCU on `/dev/ttyS1`.
>
> **Source.** Measured: under DSM, `scemd` writes `u` about every 10 s for as long as DSM runs, and DSM's shutdown service `SynoDisableFanCheck` disarms it (presumably with `t`) (`research/2026-10-23-microp-commands.md`, "Observed traffic"). The plugin copies that: `u` at start and every N s, `t` on every stop.
>
> **Default 10 s** = what DSM does. Allowed 2..60; anything else is refused by the cfg parser and logged.
>
> **Not measured.** What the MCU does when `u` stops coming (hypothesis: fan-fail beeping or full fan speed). Intervals above 10 s were never tested on the unit, so the MCU's timeout is unknown. Shorter is harmless (more bytes on a 9600 baud line that is otherwise idle).
>
> **Risk.** Raising it could let the MCU's watchdog fire, if it has one. Leave it at 10 unless you are measuring that.
:end

