:synohw_ac_plug:
> **What it does.** Keep: nothing is sent. On: `q` (`ENABLE_AC_RECOVERY`), Off: `p` (`DISABLE_AC_RECOVERY`), sent at every daemon start. The bytes are measured under DSM on this unit.
>
> **Default Keep**, so the plugin does not change what DSM last configured. When the bytes were measured, DSM had AC recovery disabled on this unit (`research/2026-10-23-microp-commands.md`, last line).
>
> **Not measured.** Whether the MCU keeps the setting through a power loss, i.e. whether the unit really powers on again: the plug was never pulled to test it.
>
> **Risk.** Low. On: the box starts by itself after a power cut (also in the middle of a UPS-triggered shutdown sequence if power returns).
:end

_(Wake-on-LAN in the MCU)_ (WOL):
: <select name="WOL" aria-label="Wake-on-LAN in the MCU (WOL)">
  <?=mk_option($cfg['WOL'], "keep", "Keep (send nothing)")?>
  <?=mk_option($cfg['WOL'], "on", "On (send l)")?>
  <?=mk_option($cfg['WOL'], "off", "Off (send k)")?>
  </select>

:synohw_wol_plug:
> **What it does.** Keep: nothing. On: `l`, Off: `k` (DSM's tool names them `ENALBE_WAKE_ON_LAN_FUNCTION` / `DISABLE_WAKE_ON_LAN_FUNCTION`, measured), sent at every daemon start.
>
> **Default Keep**: DSM had it disabled when measured.
>
> **Not measured.** Whether a magic packet wakes the unit. The plugin does not set the NIC's own wake-on-LAN (`ethtool -s ethN wol g`); both may be needed.
>
> **Risk.** None known.
:end

_(Power off in the SHUTDOWN band)_ (SHUTDOWN_ACTION):
: <select name="SHUTDOWN_ACTION" aria-label="Power off in the SHUTDOWN band (SHUTDOWN_ACTION)">
  <?=mk_option($cfg['SHUTDOWN_ACTION'], "no", "No (log only)")?>
  <?=mk_option($cfg['SHUTDOWN_ACTION'], "yes", "Yes (run /sbin/poweroff)")?>
  </select>

:synohw_shutdown_plug:
> **What it does.** scemd.xml has `action="SHUTDOWN"` rows: disk 61 °C, CPU 95 °C (scemd.xml:9, 14, 23, 28). No: the daemon logs "SHUTDOWN band reached" to syslog at every evaluation while it lasts. Yes: it marks a system shutdown and runs `/sbin/poweroff`, Unraid's normal clean shutdown: `rc.6` runs `K05synology-hardware` (fan 99 %, `t`, `5`, `7`) and then `rc.local_shutdown` stops the array (`rc.local_shutdown:28-30`).
>
> **Default No.** Unraid already warns about disk temperatures itself (Settings > Disk Settings; defaults 45 °C warning, 55 °C critical, `default.cfg:26-27`), and an unattended poweroff of a file server is a big step for a plugin to take.
>
> **Risk with Yes.** One reading at or above the band powers the server off (no repeat count, no delay), so a bogus sensor value would do it too. Never triggered on the unit: untested on hardware.
:end

_(Debug log)_ (DEBUG):
: <select name="DEBUG" aria-label="Debug log (DEBUG)">
  <?=mk_option($cfg['DEBUG'], "no", "No")?>
  <?=mk_option($cfg['DEBUG'], "yes", "Yes (every MCU byte and every command, noisy)")?>
  </select>

:synohw_debug_plug:
> **What it does.** No: each byte value the MCU sends is logged once ("first seen"), then counts every 600 s (`MCU_LOG_SEC`); commands sent are not logged. Yes: every byte read from ttyS1 (`MCU byte 0x..`) and every command sent (`sent 'u'`) goes to syslog.
>
> **Why the default is No.** The MCU streams "K3" about 4 times a second from power-on, with or without commands (measured 2026-10-24, recorded in `scripts/synohw-daemon`). With Yes that is about 8 syslog lines per second, roughly 700,000 a day, plus one per command. Unraid keeps `/var/log` in RAM.
>
> **Use it for** measuring an unknown event, e.g. which byte the power button sends (not known yet), then switch back.
>
> **Bytes sent.** None; logging only.
:end

<h3 class="synohw-sub">Network ports</h3>

_(Real MACs from the DOM)_ (MACFIX):
: <select name="MACFIX" aria-label="Real MACs from the DOM (MACFIX)">
  <?=mk_option($cfg['MACFIX'], "yes", "Yes")?>
  <?=mk_option($cfg['MACFIX'], "no", "No")?>
  </select>

:synohw_macfix_plug:
> **Why it exists.** The four Intel i354 ports (driver `igb`) carry Intel's placeholder MAC `00:a0:c9:00:00:00` to `:03` in their NVM; DSM sets the real MACs at boot from the `vender` file on the internal DOM (measured, `docs/UPSTREAM-PR-NOTES.md` M1). Without this, every DS2415+ running another OS has the same four MACs, and DHCP sees a different machine.
>
> **What Yes does.** Reads `vender` from the DOM (USB `f400:f400`, partition 2) read-only: `blockdev --setro`, `mount -o ro,noexec,nosuid,nodev`, decode, `umount`, cached for this boot in `/var/run/synology-hardware/macs`. Layout: 4 x (6-byte MAC + 1 check byte), serial at offset 32 (measured, M2). MAC n goes to the n-th PCI network function in PCI address order (assumed to be DSM's eth0..3 order, M4: not checked which RJ45 is which). It then writes `/boot/config/udev/70-synology-hardware-mac.rules` (only when the content differs) and a copy in `/etc/udev/rules.d`. At every boot Unraid copies `/boot/config/udev/*.rules` into `/etc/udev/rules.d` before udevd starts (`rc.S.cont:33-40`, udev `:51-55`), long before the network (`rc.inet1` from `rc.M:65-66`), so each port gets its real MAC as it appears. Nothing is sent to the MCU.
>
> **First Apply with placeholders still active** (or first install): the MACs are applied live once, detached: `rc.inet1 br0_stop`, `ip link set dev ethN address ...` per port, `rc.inet1 br0_start`. A few seconds without network and a new DHCP lease for the real MAC (possibly a new IP). Skipped while Docker or VMs run (deleting br0 would cut them off); then the next boot does it.
>
> **No** deletes both rule files; the ports go back to the placeholders at the next boot.
>
> **Default Yes.** **Risk:** the network drop above, once; a DHCP reservation keyed on the placeholder no longer matches.
:end

_(Orange bay LED from disk health)_ (LED_HEALTH):
: <select name="LED_HEALTH" aria-label="Orange bay LED from disk health (LED_HEALTH)">
