<div id="synohw-sweep-graphs" hidden>
<h3 class="synohw-chart-title" id="synohw-sweep-temp-h">Sweep: temperatures (&deg;C); a vertical line at each step start, the sweep's abort limits in red</h3>
<div id="synohw-sweep-temp" class="synohw-chart" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-labelledby="synohw-sweep-temp-h"></div>
<h3 class="synohw-chart-title">Sweep: fan duty sent (%)</h3>
<div id="synohw-sweep-duty" class="synohw-chart" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-label="Fan duty sent during the sweep, per step"></div>
<ul id="synohw-sweep-legend" class="synohw-sweep-legend"></ul>
<p class="grey-text whitespace-normal">From <code>/var/run/synology-hardware/sweep.csv</code> (one row every 30 s). At the cursor: <span id="synohw-sweep-at">hover or focus a sweep graph</span></p>
</div></div>

:synohw_sweep_plug:
> **What it does.** A characterisation run to find out experimentally how low the fan can go. The daemon holds the fan at one fixed duty after another (default plan: 20 baseline, 15, 12, 10, 20, 40, 70, 99 %), records every 30 s the CPU temperature (max and each core), every real disk's temperature (keyed by its serial/id, not by `sdX`) and how many MCU bytes of each value arrived, and writes a per-step summary at the end: start, end, min, max and slope in &deg;C per 10 min for the CPU and every disk, and whether the temperatures had settled. The fan speed itself cannot be read (no tach reading), so "where it fluctuates, where it really rises or falls" is judged from the temperatures and from the MCU byte counts, not from RPM.
>
> **Start and stop are on the command line only** (this panel is read-only): `/etc/rc.d/rc.synology-hardware sweep start [duty ...]`, `sweep status`, `sweep stop`. Duties are integers 10 to 99, at most 24 steps; nothing below 10 is ever accepted (the command allow-list refuses `V` below 10 as well). The running daemon does the sweep because it owns the serial port.
>
> **Timing.** Each step is held at least 600 s (`SWEEP_MIN_HOLD`), moves on early once settled (no temperature moved more than 0.5 &deg;C in the last 600 s: `SWEEP_SETTLE_WINDOW`, `SWEEP_SETTLE_DELTA`) and at most 1800 s (`SWEEP_MAX_HOLD`). Temperatures are whole degrees, so "settled" means unchanged. The sweep re-sends its duty every `FAN_REASSERT` seconds like the table duty.
>
> **Safety: any of these ends the sweep at once, puts the table duty back, logs the reason to syslog and shows ABORTED here.** A disk at 46 &deg;C or more, or the CPU at 65 &deg;C or more (the first raise thresholds of the LOW table: 40 % and 70 %, `scemd.xml:17-29`); any temperature that cannot be read (a disk asleep or without a reading counts); **any MCU byte other than `3` (0x33) and `K` (0x4b)**, the only two the MCU has been measured to send (about 4 each per second), because anything else could be a fan-fail alarm; a failed write to the serial port. Stopping or restarting the daemon, or `sweep stop`, ends it too. A sweep never survives a daemon restart or a reboot: its state is only in `/var/run/synology-hardware` (RAM).
>
> **Results.** `/var/run/synology-hardware/sweep.csv` (columns: epoch, step, duty, cpu_max, core:..., disk:&lt;id&gt;..., mcu_33, mcu_4b, mcu_other) and `sweep-summary.txt` while it runs and until reboot. At the end (done, aborted or stopped) both are copied once to `/boot/config/plugins/synology-hardware/sweep-<date>-<time>.csv` and `.txt`: one small flash write, nothing else is written to the flash during a sweep.
>
> **Sources and limits.** The 46 &deg;C and 65 &deg;C limits and the duty table are Synology's own (`capture/2026-10-23/bin/usr/syno/etc.defaults/scemd.xml`, copied in the fan help above); the 10 % floor, the 0.5 &deg;C settle rule, the hold times and the 0x33/0x4b byte rule are this plugin's choices. The MCU's K/3 stream and the `V<nn>` command are measured (`research/2026-10-23-microp-commands.md`). Not known: what the MCU does with a duty below its lowest DSM step (20 %); the sweep is the experiment that probes it, and a duty the fan cannot spin at may stall it. Temperatures react slowly (minutes), so a step shorter than the minimum hold shows little.
:end
